y and rest the back.\r\nEpidural Steroid Injections: For temporary relief of severe radicular pain.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications for Surgery:\r\n\r\nCauda Equina Syndrome (Absolute emergency: saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder incontinence).\r\nProgressive motor weakness (e.g., foot drop).\r\nFailure of conservative treatment after 6 weeks.\r\nSevere, unremitting sciatic pain.\r\nSurgical Procedures:\r\n\r\nFenestration: Removal of the ligamentum flavum to access and remove the disc.\r\nLaminectomy and Discectomy: Removal of the lamina to widely expose the spinal canal and excise the herniated disc material.\r\nMicrodiscectomy: Standard of care. Uses an operating microscope through a small incision, minimizing tissue damage and allowing faster recovery.\r\n2. Spinal Canal Stenosis\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Spinal canal stenosis is an abnormal narrowing of the central spinal canal, the lateral recesses, or the intervertebral foramina, which leads to the compression of the spinal cord (in cervical spine) or the nerve roots of the cauda equina (in lumbar spine).\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nCongenital/Developmental: Idiopathic narrowing (e.g., Achondroplasia), pedicles are congenitally short.\r\nAcquired (Most Common):\r\nDegenerative: Hypertrophy of the facet joints, thickening of the ligamentum flavum, and osteophyte formation.\r\nSpondylolisthesis / Spondylolysis: Slippage of vertebrae narrows the canal.\r\nIatrogenic: Post-laminectomy scar tissue.\r\nTraumatic: Malunited spinal fractures.\r\nMetabolic: Paget's disease.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nAging and microtrauma lead to disc degeneration. To stabilize the hypermobile segment, the body reacts by forming osteophytes (bone spurs) at the facet joints and vertebral body margins. The ligamentum flavum hypertrophies and buckles inward. This combination circumferentially tightens the space available for the neural elements. In the upright position, the lumbar spine extends, causing the ligamentum flavum to buckle further, severely compressing the roots.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nNeurogenic Intermittent Claudication (Pathognomonic):\r\nPain, heaviness, and cramping in the buttocks, thighs, and calves that appears upon walking or prolonged standing.\r\nRelief by Flexion: Symptoms are dramatically relieved by sitting, squatting, or bending forward (e.g., leaning on a shopping cart - \"Shopping Cart Sign\"). Flexion opens up the spinal canal.\r\nBack Pain: Chronic, dull ache.\r\nBilateral Leg Symptoms: Unlike PID which is usually unilateral, stenosis often causes bilateral leg weakness or paresthesia.\r\nSigns:\r\n\r\nClinical examination is often normal at rest.\r\nNeurological deficits may only appear after making the patient walk until symptoms begin (stress test).\r\nSLR is usually negative (unlike PID).\r\nVascular examination (pedal pulses) is normal (differentiates from vascular claudication).\r\nInvestigations\r\nPlain X-rays: Show degenerative changes, decreased disc height, facet hypertrophy, or degenerative spondylolisthesis.\r\nMRI (Gold Standard): Clearly demonstrates the narrowing of the dural sac. Shows the classic \"hourglass\" or \"trefoil\" appearance of the stenotic canal.\r\nCT Myelogram: Used if MRI is contraindicated or for better bony detail.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management\r\nLifestyle Modification: Avoid prolonged extension, use a walker or cart.\r\nMedications: NSAIDs, Gabapentin/Pregabalin for nerve pain.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Flexion exercises (Williams exercises) to flatten the lumbar lordosis and open the canal. Core strengthening.\r\nEpidural Steroid Injections: Can provide months of relief by reducing local root inflammation.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Severe impairment of quality of life, walking distance reduced to a few meters, or progressive neurological deficit.\r\n\r\nWide Decompressive Laminectomy: The standard procedure. The spinous processes, lamina, and hypertrophied ligamentum flavum are removed over the affected levels to \"unroof\" the spinal canal and decompress the nerves.\r\nSpinal Fusion: May be required along with decompression if there is associated instability (e.g., degenerative spondylolisthesis).\r\n3. Spondylosis (Cervical and Lumbar)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Spondylosis is a general term for age-related wear and tear (degenerative osteoarthritis) affecting the spinal discs and the zygapophyseal (facet) joints. It is characterized by disc space narrowing and the formation of osteophytes (bone spurs). It predominantly affects the most mobile segments of the spine: the lower cervical (C5-C7) and lower lumbar (L4-S1) regions.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nAgeing: Primary cause. Wear and tear over decades.\r\nMicrotrauma: Repetitive occupational stress.\r\nGenetic predisposition: Quality of collagen in the disc.\r\nPrevious trauma/surgery: Accelerates degeneration at adjacent levels.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nThe process begins with disc desiccation (loss of water content in the nucleus pulposus). The disc loses its height and its shock-absorbing capacity. The annular fibers bulge outward. Because the disc space is narrowed, abnormal stresses are transferred to the facet joints posteriorly and the vertebral body margins anteriorly. The body responds by forming osteophytes (traction spurs) at the vertebral margins and facet joints. These osteophytes can project into the spinal canal or neural foramina, compressing the spinal cord (myelopathy) or nerve roots (radiculopathy).\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nCervical Spondylosis:\r\nAxial Neck Pain: Stiffness and pain in the neck, worse in the morning.\r\nCervical Radiculopathy: Pain radiating down the arm, numbness, tingling in the fingers due to osteophytes compressing nerve roots in the foramen.\r\nCervical Myelopathy: If central osteophytes compress the spinal cord. Presents with clumsy hands, wide-based unsteady gait, and hyperreflexia.\r\nVertebrobasilar Insufficiency: Dizziness or vertigo upon turning the head due to osteophytes compressing the vertebral artery (rare).\r\nLumbar Spondylosis:\r\nMechanical Back Pain: Dull ache in the lower back, relieved by rest, aggravated by movement.\r\nStiffness: Especially in the morning, which \"loosens up\" as the day progresses.\r\nReferred pain to the buttocks, but true radicular pain is absent unless stenosis develops.\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (AP, Lateral, and Oblique):\r\nLoss of normal lordotic curve.\r\nDecreased intervertebral disc space.\r\nMarginal osteophytes (lipping).\r\nSclerosis of the endplates.\r\nOblique views show encroachment of the neural foramina.\r\nMRI: Evaluates the extent of disc degeneration (dark discs on T2), nerve root compression, and spinal cord compression.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management (Mainstay for most patients)\r\nRest & Support: Short periods of rest. Cervical collar (for neck) or lumbosacral belt (for back) during acute flares.\r\nMedications: NSAIDs, muscle relaxants.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Heat therapy (short wave diathermy), cervical/lumbar traction, followed by neck/core strengthening exercises to stabilize the spine.\r\nPostural Training: Ergonomic adjustments at work.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nSurgery is rarely indicated for pure spondylosis (axial pain). Indications: Progressive neurological deficit, intractable radiculopathy, or severe cervical myelopathy.\r\n\r\nCervical: Anterior Cervical Discectomy and Fusion (ACDF) or Posterior Laminectomy.\r\nLumbar: Decompression with or without spinal fusion.\r\n4. Spondylolysis\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Spondylolysis is a defect or stress fracture in the pars interarticularis of the vertebral arch. The pars interarticularis is the bony segment between the superior and inferior articular processes of a vertebra. It most commonly occurs at the L5 vertebra (85% of cases). It is a defect without the forward displacement of the vertebra (if displacement occurs, it becomes spondylolisthesis).\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nCongenital weakness: A congenital dysplasia of the pars makes it susceptible.\r\nStress Fracture (Most common theory): Repeated microtrauma and mechanical stress (especially repetitive hyperextension of the spine).\r\nSports: Highly prevalent in young athletes involved in gymnastics, weightlifting, fast bowling in cricket, and diving.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nThe lumbar spine endures massive shear forces, particularly at L5-S1 due to the lumbosacral angle. Repetitive extension loads the pars interarticularis. Over time, these micro-traumas outpace the body's bony repair mechanism, leading to a fatigue/stress fracture. The defect is filled with fibrous tissue (pseudoarthrosis) rather than bone.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nAsymptomatic: Many individuals have the defect without ever experiencing pain (discovered incidentally).\r\nBack Pain: Typically insidious onset of low back pain in an adolescent or young adult athlete.\r\nAggravating factors: Pain is characteristically worsened by spinal extension (bending backward) and relieved by rest.\r\nNo Neurological Deficits: Because there is no slippage, the spinal canal is intact, and nerve roots are not compressed. Radicular symptoms are absent.\r\nSigns: Mild tenderness over the L5 spinous process. Single-leg hyperextension test (Stork test) produces pain on the affected side.\r\nInvestigations\r\nPlain X-rays:\r\nLateral view: May show a lucency in the pars, but often missed.\r\nOblique view (Diagnostic): Shows the classic \"Scotty Dog with a collar\" sign. The \"collar\" represents the radiolucent defect in the pars (the neck of the dog).\r\nCT Scan: The most sensitive modality to detect the bony defect and assess if healing has occurred.\r\nBone Scan / SPECT: Used in acute stages to detect active inflammation and bone turnover at the pars before a fracture line is visible on X-ray.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management (Successful in majority)\r\nActivity Modification: Immediate cessation of the offending sports/activities (avoiding hyperextension).\r\nBracing: An anti-lordotic (Boston) brace or TLSO worn for 6-12 weeks to immobilize the spine and allow the stress fracture to heal.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Core strengthening and hamstring stretching exercises once acute pain subsides.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Persistent severe pain failing 6 months of conservative treatment.\r\n\r\nPars Repair: Direct repair of the pars defect using screws or wiring, preserving the motion of the disc segment (usually done in younger patients with normal discs).\r\nPosterolateral Fusion: Fusing L5 to S1 if there are degenerative changes.\r\n5. Spondylolisthesis\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Spondylolisthesis is the forward translation (slippage) of one vertebra (and the spine above it) over the vertebra immediately below it. The term comes from the Greek words spondylos (spine) and olisthesis (to slip). The most common location is L5 slipping over S1, or L4 slipping over L5.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Classification (Wiltse Classification)\r\nDysplastic (Congenital): Due to congenital abnormality of the upper sacrum or the neural arch of L5.\r\nIsthmic: Secondary to a defect in the pars interarticularis (spondylolysis). Most common in young adults (L5-S1).\r\nDegenerative: Due to long-standing segmental instability and osteoarthritis of the facet joints. Most common in older females (L4-L5).\r\nTraumatic: Due to an acute fracture of the neural arch (other than the pars).\r\nPathological: Due to generalized bone disease (e.g., osteoporosis, Paget's disease, tumor).\r\nPathophysiology & Grading\r\nWhen the stabilizing structures (pars, facets, or disc) fail, the shear forces of gravity on the lordotic lumbar spine push the upper vertebra forward. In isthmic types, the posterior arch is left behind, so the central canal actually widens, though the exiting nerve roots (e.g., L5 root) can get trapped in the foramina. Meyerding's Grading: Based on the percentage of forward slip of the upper vertebra on the lower vertebral body (seen on lateral X-ray).\r\n\r\nGrade I: 0-25% slip\r\nGrade II: 25-50% slip\r\nGrade III: 50-75% slip\r\nGrade IV: 75-100% slip\r\nGrade V (Spondyloptosis): >100% (the vertebra falls completely in front of the sacrum).\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nLow Back Pain: Mechanical, dull ache, worse with standing or walking.\r\nRadicular Pain (Sciatica): Due to traction or compression of the exiting nerve root in the foramen.\r\nNeurogenic Claudication: Especially in the degenerative type.\r\nSigns:\r\n\r\nStep-off Deformity: A palpable \"step\" can be felt over the spinous processes in the lower back (in isthmic type, the step is a level higher than the slip).\r\nHamstring Spasm/Tightness: Very characteristic. The patient walks with short strides and stiff knees.\r\nPhalen-Dickson Sign: A waddling, abnormal gait with a vertical pelvis (in severe grades).\r\nTransverse Abdominal Crease: Seen in severe slips as the trunk shortens.\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (Lateral View in standing position): Diagnostic. Shows the forward slip. Used to calculate Meyerding's grade and the slip angle.\r\nOblique X-rays: Shows the pars defect in isthmic types (Scotty dog with a broken collar).\r\nDynamic X-rays (Flexion/Extension): To assess for spinal instability (abnormal movement between vertebrae).\r\nMRI: To evaluate disc status, nerve root compression, and central canal dimensions.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management\r\nIndicated for Grade I and II slips with mild symptoms.\r\n\r\nRest, activity restriction (avoid heavy lifting/contact sports).\r\nNSAIDs and analgesics.\r\nLumbosacral corset for support.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Abdominal and core strengthening exercises, hamstring stretching.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Progressive slip (especially in children), Grade III or higher, severe intractable back/leg pain, neurological deficit.\r\n\r\nDecompression and Fusion (Standard of Care): The compressed nerve roots are decompressed. The unstable segment (e.g., L5-S1) is fused using bone graft (posterolateral fusion) and stabilized with titanium pedicle screws and rods.\r\nReduction: In severe grades, the slipped vertebra may be partially pulled back into alignment before fusion, though this carries a risk of nerve stretching.\r\n6. Lumbago / Lumbosacral Strain\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Lumbago is a descriptive term for pain in the lower back. \"Lumbosacral strain\" specifically refers to mechanical back pain resulting from injury, stretching, or partial tearing of the muscles, fascia, or ligaments of the lower back. It is the most common cause of acute low back pain.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nAcute Strain: Sudden, forceful lifting, especially with the spine in a twisted or awkward position. Sudden uncoordinated movement or a fall.\r\nChronic Strain: Poor posture, prolonged sitting or standing in unergonomic positions, obesity (increased lumbar lordosis), weak core muscles, or wearing high heels.\r\nPsychological factors: Stress and depression can manifest as chronic muscular back pain.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nThe spinal column relies on a complex network of muscles (erector spinae) and ligaments (supraspinous, interspinous) for stability. When demand exceeds capacity, micro-tears occur in these soft tissues. This leads to localized bleeding, inflammation, and local swelling. The body responds with a reflex muscle spasm to splint and protect the injured area, which in itself is highly painful and restricts movement.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nPain: Acute, sharp, or dull aching pain localized to the lower back.\r\nOnset: Often sudden after a specific event (e.g., \"I lifted a heavy box and felt a pop\"), but can be gradual in chronic strain.\r\nRadiation: The pain may radiate to the buttocks or posterior thigh, but does not go below the knee (non-radicular).\r\nStiffness: Severe difficulty in bending forward or getting out of bed.\r\nSigns:\r\n\r\nPosture: The patient may stand with a tilt or flattened lumbar lordosis to avoid stretching the injured muscle.\r\nTenderness: Diffuse, localized tenderness over the paraspinal muscles.\r\nMuscle Spasm: Visible or palpable tight bands of muscle beside the spine.\r\nNeurological Exam: Completely normal (normal power, reflexes, and sensation). SLR is negative (though it may cause back tightness).\r\nInvestigations\r\nThe diagnosis is primarily clinical.\r\n\r\nX-rays (AP and Lateral): Usually completely normal. May show a straightening of the normal lumbar lordosis due to muscle spasm. Ordered primarily to rule out \"red flag\" conditions like fractures, tumors, or infections.\r\nMRI: Not indicated for acute lumbago unless symptoms persist beyond 4-6 weeks or neurological signs develop.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management (Curative in almost all cases)\r\nShort-term Rest: Bed rest for 1-2 days on a firm mattress. Prolonged bed rest is discouraged as it leads to muscle deconditioning.\r\nEarly Mobilization: The patient is encouraged to walk and resume light activities as soon as tolerated.\r\nMedications:\r\nAnalgesics (Paracetamol).\r\nNSAIDs (Ibuprofen, Diclofenac) to reduce inflammation.\r\nMuscle relaxants (Thiocolchicoside) to break the pain-spasm cycle.\r\nLocal Therapy: Ice packs in the first 48 hours to reduce inflammation, followed by heat therapy to relax muscles.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Back extension exercises, core strengthening, and posture correction once the acute phase passes. Patient education on proper lifting techniques (lift with knees, not back).\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nThere is no role for surgery in a pure lumbosacral muscle strain.\r\n7. Sacralisation (Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra - LSTV)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Sacralisation is a congenital anomaly where the lowest (fifth) lumbar vertebra (L5) is partially or completely fused with the first sacral segment (S1) or the iliac crest. It is a type of Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra (LSTV). Because L5 takes on the characteristics of the sacrum, the lumbar spine appears to have only four mobile vertebrae.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nCongenital: It occurs during embryonic development due to a failure of segmentation of the somites.\r\nGenetic predisposition: Often runs in families, representing a variant of normal spinal development.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nSacralisation can be unilateral or bilateral, and the fusion can be bony (complete) or fibrous (pseudoarthrosis). Biomechanical Alteration: Normally, L5-S1 is a highly mobile segment that absorbs a lot of stress. When L5 is fused to the sacrum, this segment becomes rigid. Consequently, the mechanical stress of spinal movement is transferred to the level above (L4-L5). Over time, this increased stress at L4-L5 leads to accelerated disc degeneration, hypermobility, and facet arthropathy at that specific level (a condition known as Bertolotti's Syndrome). Additionally, a pseudoarthrosis between a large L5 transverse process and the sacrum can become a direct source of localized pain.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nOften Asymptomatic: Found incidentally on X-rays taken for other reasons.\r\nLow Back Pain: If symptomatic, patients present with mechanical low back pain.\r\nBertolotti's Syndrome: Back pain with or without radiculopathy originating from the hypermobile L4-L5 level above the sacralisation.\r\nLocalized Pain: Unilateral pain if there is inflammation at the site of a pseudoarthrosis between the transverse process and sacrum.\r\nSigns: May have restricted spinal mobility. Local tenderness over the lumbosacral junction. Neurological exam is typically normal.\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-ray (AP view of Lumbosacral spine): Diagnostic. Evaluated using the Castellvi classification:\r\nBroadening of the L5 transverse process (>19mm).\r\nPseudoarthrosis with the sacrum/ilium.\r\nComplete bony fusion with the sacrum.\r\nMRI: Ordered if disc herniation is suspected at the level above (L4-L5).\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management\r\nReassurance: The patient should be reassured that it is a common congenital variant and not a disease.\r\nSymptomatic relief: Analgesics and NSAIDs for acute pain.\r\nPhysiotherapy: Core strengthening and pelvic tilt exercises to improve spinal biomechanics and protect the L4-L5 level.\r\nLocal Injections: A steroid injection into the pseudoarthrosis (if present) can provide diagnostic confirmation and therapeutic relief.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Very rare. Considered only if conservative treatment fails and the pain is severely debilitating.\r\n\r\nExcision: Resection of the enlarged transverse process or pseudoarthrosis.\r\nSpinal Fusion: Fusion of the L4-L5 segment if it is severely degenerated and unstable.\r\n8. Lumbarisation (Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra - LSTV)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Lumbarisation is a congenital anomaly where the first sacral segment (S1) fails to fuse completely with the rest of the sacrum. As a result, S1 acts like a separate, mobile vertebra, taking on the characteristics of a lumbar vertebra. The patient effectively appears to have a \"sixth\" lumbar vertebra and a shortened sacrum.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nCongenital: Arises from a defect in embryonic segmentation. It is the anatomical opposite of sacralisation, though they are both classed as transitional vertebrae.\r\nPathophysiology\r\nLike sacralisation, lumbarisation alters the normal biomechanics of the lower spine. The extra mobile segment can lead to instability. The \"new\" L6-S2 joint (or what was supposed to be S1-S2) is not designed to handle the mechanical loads and shear forces of a lumbar disc. This can lead to premature degenerative changes, facet arthritis, and disc prolapse at this transitional level or the levels above it.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nAsymptomatic: Mostly an incidental finding.\r\nMechanical Back Pain: Due to altered mechanics, instability, or early degenerative changes in the extra mobile segment.\r\nSigns: No specific clinical signs differentiate it from standard lumbago without X-rays. Neurological exam is typically normal.\r\nInvestigations\r\nPlain X-rays (AP and Lateral): Counting the vertebrae from the lower ribs downwards reveals six lumbar-type vertebrae that are not fused to the sacrum.\r\nMRI: To assess disc health at the transitional segments if the patient has radicular symptoms.\r\nManagement\r\nThe management principles are identical to sacralisation.\r\n\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management\r\nReassurance.\r\nAnalgesics, NSAIDs during painful episodes.\r\nPhysiotherapy focusing on core stability and back muscle strengthening to support the extra mobile segment.\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nExtremely rare. Only indicated if there is a prolapsed disc or severe instability causing neurological compression at the transitional level, requiring decompression and fusion.\r\n9. Coccydynia\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Coccydynia is a painful condition localized to the coccyx (tailbone) and the surrounding sacrococcygeal region.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nDirect Trauma (Most Common): A fall directly onto the buttocks (e.g., falling on ice, falling down stairs), which can result in a contusion, sprain, or fracture-dislocation of the sacrococcygeal joint.\r\nChildbirth: Vaginal delivery can stretch or injure the coccyx as the baby's head passes through the pelvis.\r\nRepetitive Strain: Prolonged sitting on hard, narrow, or uncomfortable surfaces (e.g., cycling).\r\nIdiopathic: In some cases, no specific cause is found (could be due to degenerative changes or bony spurs).\r\nPathophysiology\r\nThe sacrococcygeal joint normally has a small amount of movement (flexion and extension). Trauma causes a sprain or partial tear of the sacrococcygeal ligaments, leading to hypermobility or subluxation of the joint. This creates chronic inflammation. The pain is exacerbated when the patient sits, as the weight forces the coccyx into extension, pulling on the inflamed ligaments.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nLocalized Pain: Severe pain directly over the tailbone.\r\nSitting Pain: Exacerbated by sitting, especially leaning backward on a hard surface.\r\nTransitional Pain: Sharp pain when moving from a sitting to a standing position (as the pelvic muscles attached to the coccyx contract).\r\nPain with Defecation: May occur due to pressure from the rectum.\r\nSigns:\r\n\r\nPoint Tenderness: Exquisite tenderness precisely over the coccyx or the sacrococcygeal joint when palpated from the outside.\r\nPer Rectal (PR) Examination: If required, the coccyx can be grasped between the thumb outside and index finger inside the rectum; moving it elicits severe pain (confirms the diagnosis).\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (Lateral View): Shows the shape and angulation of the coccyx. May reveal a fracture, anterior subluxation, or a bony spur. (However, often X-rays are normal).\r\nDynamic X-rays (Sitting vs. Standing): Can demonstrate abnormal hypermobility of the sacrococcygeal joint when weight is applied.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management (Usually successful)\r\nCushions: The most important treatment. Use of a \"donut\" cushion or a U-shaped coccyx cushion to offload weight from the tailbone while sitting.\r\nPostural modification: Leaning slightly forward while sitting.\r\nMedications: NSAIDs for pain and inflammation.\r\nHeat Therapy: Hot sitz baths or local heating pads.\r\nLocal Injections: Injection of a local anesthetic mixed with a corticosteroid directly into the sacrococcygeal joint or around the painful ligaments. Provides excellent relief in refractory cases.\r\nManipulation: Gentle manual manipulation of the coccyx via the rectum under anesthesia (rarely done now).\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Chronic, intractable coccydynia that has failed months of aggressive conservative treatment.\r\n\r\nCoccygectomy: Surgical excision of the mobile portion of the coccyx. (Care must be taken to preserve the sphincter muscles).\r\n10. Hemivertebra\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: A hemivertebra is a severe congenital anomaly of the spine where only one half (one side) of a vertebral body develops. It acts like a wedge inserted into one side of the spinal column, leading to a congenital spinal deformity, most commonly congenital scoliosis (lateral curvature) or kyphoscoliosis.\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nEmbryological Failure: Occurs during the first 6 weeks of gestation. It is caused by the failure of formation of one of the two lateral chondrification centers of the vertebral body.\r\nOften associated with other congenital anomalies (VACTERL association: Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheal, Esophageal, Renal, and Limb defects).\r\nPathophysiology\r\nA normal vertebra is shaped like a block, keeping the spine straight. A hemivertebra is wedge-shaped. Because one side of the spine has extra bony material (the hemivertebra) and the other side is deficient, the spine curves away from the side of the hemivertebra. Furthermore, the hemivertebra has its own growth plates. As the child grows, the hemivertebra grows asymmetrically, acting as a progressive wedge that continuously worsens the scoliotic curve.\r\n\r\nClassification of Hemivertebrae\r\nFully Segmented: Has normal discs above and below it. High risk of progression because both growth plates are active.\r\nSemi-segmented: Fused to the vertebra above or below. Moderate risk of progression.\r\nNon-segmented: Fused to both the vertebra above and below. Lowest risk of progression.\r\nIncarcerated: The hemivertebra is tucked into the spine without disrupting the overall alignment much.\r\nClinical Features\r\nSpinal Deformity: The parents usually notice a lump on the back, uneven shoulders, or a visible curve in the spine (scoliosis) in infancy or early childhood.\r\nProgression: The deformity worsens rapidly during periods of growth spurts.\r\nNeurological symptoms: Usually absent initially. Severe, neglected curves can eventually cause spinal cord tethering or compression.\r\nSigns: Asymmetrical trunk, rib hump on forward bending test (Adams test).\r\nInvestigations\r\nX-rays (AP and Lateral of whole spine): Diagnostic. Shows the wedge-shaped anomalous bone and the degree of scoliosis (measured by Cobb's angle).\r\nMRI of the Spine: Mandatory to rule out associated neural axis abnormalities like tethered cord, syringomyelia, or diastematomyelia, which are common with congenital spine defects.\r\nUltrasound (Renal and Cardiac): To rule out associated VACTERL anomalies.\r\nManagement\r\nBecause congenital curves are rigid and do not respond to bracing, management is largely observational or surgical.\r\n\r\nA. Conservative Management (Observation)\r\nIndicated for non-segmented or incarcerated hemivertebrae that are not progressing.\r\nSerial X-rays every 6 months to monitor for curve progression. (Braces are ineffective for congenital scoliosis).\r\nB. Surgical Management\r\nIndications: Documented progression of the curve, fully segmented hemivertebrae in young children, or severe curves at presentation.\r\n\r\nHemivertebra Excision: The anomalous wedge of bone is surgically completely removed (via an anterior, posterior, or combined approach).\r\nShort Segment Spinal Fusion: After excision, the spine above and below the defect is brought together, straightened, and fused using pedicle screws and rods. This corrects the deformity and prevents further asymmetric growth.\r\nIn Situ Fusion: Fusing the spine as it is (without excising the hemivertebra) in very young children to arrest the growth and stop the progression of the curve.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:02:32+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":9,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:32:32Z","content":"**1 Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc PID** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc PID is defined as the herniation or extrusion of the nucleus pulposus through a rent or tear in the surrounding annulus fibrosus leading to compression of the adjacent neural structures spinal nerve roots or spinal cord It most commonly occurs in the lower lumbar spine L4 L5 and L5 S1 due to high mechanical stress but can also occur in the cervical spine C5 C6 C6 C7 **Aetiology / Causes** Age Most common in the 3rd and 4th decades of life when the nucleus is still gelatinous but the annulus has begun to weaken Trauma A sudden lifting strain twisting motion or fall Degeneration Age related desiccation drying out and weakening of the annulus fibrosus Occupational hazards Heavy manual labor repetitive bending or prolonged driving **Pathophysiology and Stages of Herniation** The disc undergoes degenerative changes A sudden mechanical load causes the semi liquid nucleus pulposus to push through weakened annular fibers Stages of PID Bulging Disc The annulus remains intact but bulges outward Protrusion The nucleus pushes into the outer layers of the annulus but is still contained Extrusion The annulus ruptures completely and the nucleus material escapes into the epidural space but remains connected to the central disc Sequestration The extruded nuclear material breaks off entirely and becomes a free fragment in the spinal canal **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Low Back Pain Usually the first symptom sudden in onset described as a catch in the back Sciatica Radicular Pain Pain radiating down the buttock posterior thigh calf and into the foot corresponding to the compressed nerve roots dermatome Neurological Symptoms Tingling numbness paraesthesia or weakness in the leg/foot Aggravating Factors Pain worsens with coughing sneezing or straining increases intraspinal pressure **Signs On Examination** Posture Loss of lumbar lordosis scoliosis sciatic tilt Spinal Movements Markedly restricted especially forward flexion Straight Leg Raising SLR Test Positive pain radiating down the leg at an angle less than 60 degrees Lasegues Test Positive Neurological Deficits L4 Root L3 L4 Disc Weak quadriceps decreased knee jerk sensory loss over medial leg L5 Root L4 L5 Disc Weak Extensor Hallucis Longus EHL normal reflexes sensory loss over first web space S1 Root L5 S1 Disc Weak plantar flexion absent ankle jerk sensory loss over lateral border of foot **Investigations** Plain X rays AP and Lateral views Usually normal in early stages May show loss of lumbar lordosis or decreased disc space Mainly done to rule out bony pathologies infection tumor MRI Investigation of Choice Accurately visualizes the soft tissues confirms the level size and location of the prolapsed disc and the degree of nerve root compression CT Scan Useful if MRI is contraindicated shows bony anatomy well **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Successful in 90 percent of cases Indicated for patients without severe progressive neurological deficits Rest Strict bed rest on a hard bed for 1 2 weeks Medications Analgesics NSAIDs for inflammation muscle relaxants for spasm and sometimes short course oral corticosteroids Physical Therapy Initially heat/cryotherapy Once acute pain subsides lumbar isometric extension exercises McKenzie exercises Lumbosacral Corset To restrict mobility and rest the back Epidural Steroid Injections For temporary relief of severe radicular pain **B Surgical Management** Indications for Surgery Cauda Equina Syndrome Absolute emergency saddle anesthesia bowel/bladder incontinence Progressive motor weakness e g foot drop Failure of conservative treatment after 6 weeks Severe unremitting sciatic pain Surgical Procedures Fenestration Removal of the ligamentum flavum to access and remove the disc Laminectomy and Discectomy Removal of the lamina to widely expose the spinal canal and excise the herniated disc material Microdiscectomy Standard of care Uses an operating microscope through a small incision minimizing tissue damage and allowing faster recovery --- **2 Spinal Canal Stenosis** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Spinal canal stenosis is an abnormal narrowing of the central spinal canal the lateral recesses or the intervertebral foramina which leads to the compression of the spinal cord in cervical spine or the nerve roots of the cauda equina in lumbar spine **Aetiology / Causes** Congenital/Developmental Idiopathic narrowing e g Achondroplasia pedicles are congenitally short Acquired Most Common Degenerative Hypertrophy of the facet joints thickening of the ligamentum flavum and osteophyte formation Spondylolisthesis / Spondylolysis Slippage of vertebrae narrows the canal Iatrogenic Post laminectomy scar tissue Traumatic Malunited spinal fractures Metabolic Pagets disease **Pathophysiology** Aging and microtrauma lead to disc degeneration To stabilize the hypermobile segment the body reacts by forming osteophytes bone spurs at the facet joints and vertebral body margins The ligamentum flavum hypertrophies and buckles inward This combination circumferentially tightens the space available for the neural elements In the upright position the lumbar spine extends causing the ligamentum flavum to buckle further severely compressing the roots **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Neurogenic Intermittent Claudication Pathognomonic Pain heaviness and cramping in the buttocks thighs and calves that appears upon walking or prolonged standing Relief by Flexion Symptoms are dramatically relieved by sitting squatting or bending forward e g leaning on a shopping cart Shopping Cart Sign Flexion opens up the spinal canal Back Pain Chronic dull ache Bilateral Leg Symptoms Unlike PID which is usually unilateral stenosis often causes bilateral leg weakness or paresthesia **Signs** Clinical examination is often normal at rest Neurological deficits may only appear after making the patient walk until symptoms begin stress test SLR is usually negative unlike PID Vascular examination pedal pulses is normal differentiates from vascular claudication **Investigations** Plain X rays Show degenerative changes decreased disc height facet hypertrophy or degenerative spondylolisthesis MRI Gold Standard Clearly demonstrates the narrowing of the dural sac Shows the classic hourglass or trefoil appearance of the stenotic canal CT Myelogram Used if MRI is contraindicated or for better bony detail **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Lifestyle Modification Avoid prolonged extension use a walker or cart Medications NSAIDs Gabapentin/Pregabalin for nerve pain Physiotherapy Flexion exercises Williams exercises to flatten the lumbar lordosis and open the canal Core strengthening Epidural Steroid Injections Can provide months of relief by reducing local root inflammation **B Surgical Management** Indications Severe impairment of quality of life walking distance reduced to a few meters or progressive neurological deficit Wide Decompressive Laminectomy The standard procedure The spinous processes lamina and hypertrophied ligamentum flavum are removed over the affected levels to unroof the spinal canal and decompress the nerves Spinal Fusion May be required along with decompression if there is associated instability e g degenerative spondylolisthesis --- **3 Spondylosis Cervical and Lumbar** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Spondylosis is a general term for age related wear and tear degenerative osteoarthritis affecting the spinal discs and the zygapophyseal facet joints It is characterized by disc space narrowing and the formation of osteophytes bone spurs It predominantly affects the most mobile segments of the spine the lower cervical C5 C7 and lower lumbar L4 S1 regions **Aetiology / Causes** Ageing Primary cause Wear and tear over decades Microtrauma Repetitive occupational stress Genetic predisposition Quality of collagen in the disc Previous trauma/surgery Accelerates degeneration at adjacent levels **Pathophysiology** The process begins with disc desiccation loss of water content in the nucleus pulposus The disc loses its height and its shock absorbing capacity The annular fibers bulge outward Because the disc space is narrowed abnormal stresses are transferred to the facet joints posteriorly and the vertebral body margins anteriorly The body responds by forming osteophytes traction spurs at the vertebral margins and facet joints These osteophytes can project into the spinal canal or neural foramina compressing the spinal cord myelopathy or nerve roots radiculopathy **Clinical Features** Cervical Spondylosis Axial Neck Pain Stiffness and pain in the neck worse in the morning Cervical Radiculopathy Pain radiating down the arm numbness tingling in the fingers due to osteophytes compressing nerve roots in the foramen Cervical Myelopathy If central osteophytes compress the spinal cord Presents with clumsy hands wide based unsteady gait and hyperreflexia Vertebrobasilar Insufficiency Dizziness or vertigo upon turning the head due to osteophytes compressing the vertebral artery rare Lumbar Spondylosis Mechanical Back Pain Dull ache in the lower back relieved by rest aggravated by movement Stiffness Especially in the morning which loosens up as the day progresses Referred pain to the buttocks but true radicular pain is absent unless stenosis develops **Investigations** X rays AP Lateral and Oblique Loss of normal lordotic curve Decreased intervertebral disc space Marginal osteophytes lipping Sclerosis of the endplates Oblique views show encroachment of the neural foramina MRI Evaluates the extent of disc degeneration dark discs on T2 nerve root compression and spinal cord compression **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Mainstay for most patients Rest and Support Short periods of rest Cervical collar for neck or lumbosacral belt for back during acute flares Medications NSAIDs muscle relaxants Physiotherapy Heat therapy short wave diathermy cervical/lumbar traction followed by neck/core strengthening exercises to stabilize the spine Postural Training Ergonomic adjustments at work **B Surgical Management** Surgery is rarely indicated for pure spondylosis axial pain Indications Progressive neurological deficit intractable radiculopathy or severe cervical myelopathy Cervical Anterior Cervical Discectomy and Fusion ACDF or Posterior Laminectomy Lumbar Decompression with or without spinal fusion --- **4 Spondylolysis** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Spondylolysis is a defect or stress fracture in the pars interarticularis of the vertebral arch The pars interarticularis is the bony segment between the superior and inferior articular processes of a vertebra It most commonly occurs at the L5 vertebra 85 percent of cases It is a defect without the forward displacement of the vertebra if displacement occurs it becomes spondylolisthesis **Aetiology / Causes** Congenital weakness A congenital dysplasia of the pars makes it susceptible Stress Fracture Most common theory Repeated microtrauma and mechanical stress especially repetitive hyperextension of the spine Sports Highly prevalent in young athletes involved in gymnastics weightlifting fast bowling in cricket and diving **Pathophysiology** The lumbar spine endures massive shear forces particularly at L5 S1 due to the lumbosacral angle Repetitive extension loads the pars interarticularis Over time these micro traumas outpace the bodys bony repair mechanism leading to a fatigue/stress fracture The defect is filled with fibrous tissue pseudoarthrosis rather than bone **Clinical Features** Asymptomatic Many individuals have the defect without ever experiencing pain discovered incidentally Back Pain Typically insidious onset of low back pain in an adolescent or young adult athlete Aggravating factors Pain is characteristically worsened by spinal extension bending backward and relieved by rest No Neurological Deficits Because there is no slippage the spinal canal is intact and nerve roots are not compressed Radicular symptoms are absent Signs Mild tenderness over the L5 spinous process Single leg hyperextension test Stork test produces pain on the affected side **Investigations** Plain X rays Lateral view May show a lucency in the pars but often missed Oblique view Diagnostic Shows the classic Scotty Dog with a collar sign The collar represents the radiolucent defect in the pars the neck of the dog CT Scan The most sensitive modality to detect the bony defect and assess if healing has occurred Bone Scan / SPECT Used in acute stages to detect active inflammation and bone turnover at the pars before a fracture line is visible on X ray **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Successful in majority Activity Modification Immediate cessation of the offending sports/activities avoiding hyperextension Bracing An anti lordotic Boston brace or TLSO worn for 6 12 weeks to immobilize the spine and allow the stress fracture to heal Physiotherapy Core strengthening and hamstring stretching exercises once acute pain subsides **B Surgical Management** Indications Persistent severe pain failing 6 months of conservative treatment Pars Repair Direct repair of the pars defect using screws or wiring preserving the motion of the disc segment usually done in younger patients with normal discs Posterolateral Fusion Fusing L5 to S1 if there are degenerative changes --- **5 Spondylolisthesis** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Spondylolisthesis is the forward translation slippage of one vertebra and the spine above it over the vertebra immediately below it The term comes from the Greek words spondylos spine and olisthesis to slip The most common location is L5 slipping over S1 or L4 slipping over L5 **Aetiology / Classification** Wiltse Classification Dysplastic Congenital Due to congenital abnormality of the upper sacrum or the neural arch of L5 Isthmic Secondary to a defect in the pars interarticularis spondylolysis Most common in young adults L5 S1 Degenerative Due to long standing segmental instability and osteoarthritis of the facet joints Most common in older females L4 L5 Traumatic Due to an acute fracture of the neural arch other than the pars Pathological Due to generalized bone disease e g osteoporosis Pagets disease tumor **Pathophysiology and Grading** When the stabilizing structures pars facets or disc fail the shear forces of gravity on the lordotic lumbar spine push the upper vertebra forward In isthmic types the posterior arch is left behind so the central canal actually widens though the exiting nerve roots e g L5 root can get trapped in the foramina Meyerdings Grading Based on the percentage of forward slip of the upper vertebra on the lower vertebral body seen on lateral X ray Grade I 0 25 percent slip Grade II 25 50 percent slip Grade III 50 75 percent slip Grade IV 75 100 percent slip Grade V Spondyloptosis greater than 100 percent the vertebra falls completely in front of the sacrum **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Low Back Pain Mechanical dull ache worse with standing or walking Radicular Pain Sciatica Due to traction or compression of the exiting nerve root in the foramen Neurogenic Claudication Especially in the degenerative type **Signs** Step off Deformity A palpable step can be felt over the spinous processes in the lower back in isthmic type the step is a level higher than the slip Hamstring Spasm/Tightness Very characteristic The patient walks with short strides and stiff knees Phalen Dickson Sign A waddling abnormal gait with a vertical pelvis in severe grades Transverse Abdominal Crease Seen in severe slips as the trunk shortens **Investigations** X rays Lateral View in standing position Diagnostic Shows the forward slip Used to calculate Meyerdings grade and the slip angle Oblique X rays Shows the pars defect in isthmic types Scotty dog with a broken collar Dynamic X rays Flexion/Extension To assess for spinal instability abnormal movement between vertebrae MRI To evaluate disc status nerve root compression and central canal dimensions **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Indicated for Grade I and II slips with mild symptoms Rest activity restriction avoid heavy lifting/contact sports NSAIDs and analgesics Lumbosacral corset for support Physiotherapy Abdominal and core strengthening exercises hamstring stretching **B Surgical Management** Indications Progressive slip especially in children Grade III or higher severe intractable back/leg pain neurological deficit Decompression and Fusion Standard of Care The compressed nerve roots are decompressed The unstable segment e g L5 S1 is fused using bone graft posterolateral fusion and stabilized with titanium pedicle screws and rods Reduction In severe grades the slipped vertebra may be partially pulled back into alignment before fusion though this carries a risk of nerve stretching --- **6 Lumbago / Lumbosacral Strain** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Lumbago is a descriptive term for pain in the lower back Lumbosacral strain specifically refers to mechanical back pain resulting from injury stretching or partial tearing of the muscles fascia or ligaments of the lower back It is the most common cause of acute low back pain **Aetiology / Causes** Acute Strain Sudden forceful lifting especially with the spine in a twisted or awkward position Sudden uncoordinated movement or a fall Chronic Strain Poor posture prolonged sitting or standing in unergonomic positions obesity increased lumbar lordosis weak core muscles or wearing high heels Psychological factors Stress and depression can manifest as chronic muscular back pain **Pathophysiology** The spinal column relies on a complex network of muscles erector spinae and ligaments supraspinous interspinous for stability When demand exceeds capacity micro tears occur in these soft tissues This leads to localized bleeding inflammation and local swelling The body responds with a reflex muscle spasm to splint and protect the injured area which in itself is highly painful and restricts movement **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Pain Acute sharp or dull aching pain localized to the lower back Onset Often sudden after a specific event e g I lifted a heavy box and felt a pop but can be gradual in chronic strain Radiation The pain may radiate to the buttocks or posterior thigh but does not go below the knee non radicular Stiffness Severe difficulty in bending forward or getting out of bed **Signs** Posture The patient may stand with a tilt or flattened lumbar lordosis to avoid stretching the injured muscle Tenderness Diffuse localized tenderness over the paraspinal muscles Muscle Spasm Visible or palpable tight bands of muscle beside the spine Neurological Exam Completely normal normal power reflexes and sensation SLR is negative though it may cause back tightness **Investigations** The diagnosis is primarily clinical X rays AP and Lateral Usually completely normal May show a straightening of the normal lumbar lordosis due to muscle spasm Ordered primarily to rule out red flag conditions like fractures tumors or infections MRI Not indicated for acute lumbago unless symptoms persist beyond 4 6 weeks or neurological signs develop **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Curative in almost all cases Short term Rest Bed rest for 1 2 days on a firm mattress Prolonged bed rest is discouraged as it leads to muscle deconditioning Early Mobilization The patient is encouraged to walk and resume light activities as soon as tolerated Medications Analgesics Paracetamol NSAIDs Ibuprofen Diclofenac to reduce inflammation Muscle relaxants Thiocolchicoside to break the pain spasm cycle Local Therapy Ice packs in the first 48 hours to reduce inflammation followed by heat therapy to relax muscles Physiotherapy Back extension exercises core strengthening and posture correction once the acute phase passes Patient education on proper lifting techniques lift with knees not back **B Surgical Management** There is no role for surgery in a pure lumbosacral muscle strain --- **7 Sacralisation Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra LSTV** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Sacralisation is a congenital anomaly where the lowest fifth lumbar vertebra L5 is partially or completely fused with the first sacral segment S1 or the iliac crest It is a type of Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra LSTV Because L5 takes on the characteristics of the sacrum the lumbar spine appears to have only four mobile vertebrae **Aetiology / Causes** Congenital It occurs during embryonic development due to a failure of segmentation of the somites Genetic predisposition Often runs in families representing a variant of normal spinal development **Pathophysiology** Sacralisation can be unilateral or bilateral and the fusion can be bony complete or fibrous pseudoarthrosis Biomechanical Alteration Normally L5 S1 is a highly mobile segment that absorbs a lot of stress When L5 is fused to the sacrum this segment becomes rigid Consequently the mechanical stress of spinal movement is transferred to the level above L4 L5 Over time this increased stress at L4 L5 leads to accelerated disc degeneration hypermobility and facet arthropathy at that specific level a condition known as Bertolottis Syndrome Additionally a pseudoarthrosis between a large L5 transverse process and the sacrum can become a direct source of localized pain **Clinical Features** Often Asymptomatic Found incidentally on X rays taken for other reasons Low Back Pain If symptomatic patients present with mechanical low back pain Bertolottis Syndrome Back pain with or without radiculopathy originating from the hypermobile L4 L5 level above the sacralisation Localized Pain Unilateral pain if there is inflammation at the site of a pseudoarthrosis between the transverse process and sacrum Signs May have restricted spinal mobility Local tenderness over the lumbosacral junction Neurological exam is typically normal **Investigations** X ray AP view of Lumbosacral spine Diagnostic Evaluated using the Castellvi classification Broadening of the L5 transverse process greater than 19mm Pseudoarthrosis with the sacrum/ilium Complete bony fusion with the sacrum MRI Ordered if disc herniation is suspected at the level above L4 L5 **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Reassurance The patient should be reassured that it is a common congenital variant and not a disease Symptomatic relief Analgesics and NSAIDs for acute pain Physiotherapy Core strengthening and pelvic tilt exercises to improve spinal biomechanics and protect the L4 L5 level Local Injections A steroid injection into the pseudoarthrosis if present can provide diagnostic confirmation and therapeutic relief **B Surgical Management** Indications Very rare Considered only if conservative treatment fails and the pain is severely debilitating Excision Resection of the enlarged transverse process or pseudoarthrosis Spinal Fusion Fusion of the L4 L5 segment if it is severely degenerated and unstable --- **8 Lumbarisation Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra LSTV** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Lumbarisation is a congenital anomaly where the first sacral segment S1 fails to fuse completely with the rest of the sacrum As a result S1 acts like a separate mobile vertebra taking on the characteristics of a lumbar vertebra The patient effectively appears to have a sixth lumbar vertebra and a shortened sacrum **Aetiology / Causes** Congenital Arises from a defect in embryonic segmentation It is the anatomical opposite of sacralisation though they are both classed as transitional vertebrae **Pathophysiology** Like sacralisation lumbarisation alters the normal biomechanics of the lower spine The extra mobile segment can lead to instability The new L6 S2 joint or what was supposed to be S1 S2 is not designed to handle the mechanical loads and shear forces of a lumbar disc This can lead to premature degenerative changes facet arthritis and disc prolapse at this transitional level or the levels above it **Clinical Features** Asymptomatic Mostly an incidental finding Mechanical Back Pain Due to altered mechanics instability or early degenerative changes in the extra mobile segment Signs No specific clinical signs differentiate it from standard lumbago without X rays Neurological exam is typically normal **Investigations** Plain X rays AP and Lateral Counting the vertebrae from the lower ribs downwards reveals six lumbar type vertebrae that are not fused to the sacrum MRI To assess disc health at the transitional segments if the patient has radicular symptoms **Management** The management principles are identical to sacralisation **A Conservative / Medical Management** Reassurance Analgesics NSAIDs during painful episodes Physiotherapy focusing on core stability and back muscle strengthening to support the extra mobile segment **B Surgical Management** Extremely rare Only indicated if there is a prolapsed disc or severe instability causing neurological compression at the transitional level requiring decompression and fusion --- **9 Coccydynia** **Introduction and Definition** Definition Coccydynia is a painful condition localized to the coccyx tailbone and the surrounding sacrococcygeal region **Aetiology / Causes** Direct Trauma Most Common A fall directly onto the buttocks e g falling on ice falling down stairs which can result in a contusion sprain or fracture dislocation of the sacrococcygeal joint Childbirth Vaginal delivery can stretch or injure the coccyx as the babys head passes through the pelvis Repetitive Strain Prolonged sitting on hard narrow or uncomfortable surfaces e g cycling Idiopathic In some cases no specific cause is found could be due to degenerative changes or bony spurs **Pathophysiology** The sacrococcygeal joint normally has a small amount of movement flexion and extension Trauma causes a sprain or partial tear of the sacrococcygeal ligaments leading to hypermobility or subluxation of the joint This creates chronic inflammation The pain is exacerbated when the patient sits as the weight forces the coccyx into extension pulling on the inflamed ligaments **Clinical Features** **Symptoms** Localized Pain Severe pain directly over the tailbone Sitting Pain Exacerbated by sitting especially leaning backward on a hard surface Transitional Pain Sharp pain when moving from a sitting to a standing position as the pelvic muscles attached to the coccyx contract Pain with Defecation May occur due to pressure from the rectum **Signs** Point Tenderness Exquisite tenderness precisely over the coccyx or the sacrococcygeal joint when palpated from the outside Per Rectal PR Examination If required the coccyx can be grasped between the thumb outside and index finger inside the rectum moving it elicits severe pain confirms the diagnosis **Investigations** X rays Lateral View Shows the shape and angulation of the coccyx May reveal a fracture anterior subluxation or a bony spur However often X rays are normal Dynamic X rays Sitting vs Standing Can demonstrate abnormal hypermobility of the sacrococcygeal joint when weight is applied **Management** **A Conservative / Medical Management** Usually successful Cushions The most important treatment Use of a donut cushion or a U shaped coccyx cushion to offload weight from the tailbone while sitting Postural modification Leaning slightly forward while sitting Medications NSAIDs for pain and inflammation Heat Therapy Hot sitz baths or local heating pads Local Injections Injection of a local anesthetic mixed with a corticosteroid directly into the sacrococcygeal joint or around the painful ligaments Provides excellent relief in refractory cases Manipulation Gentle manual manipulation of the coccyx via the rectum under anesthesia rarely done now **B Surgical Management** Indications Chronic intractable coccydynia that has failed months of aggressive conservative treatment Coccygectomy Surgical excision of the mobile portion of the coccyx Care must be taken to preserve the sphincter muscles --- **10 Hemivertebra** **Introduction and Definition** Definition A hemivertebra is a severe congenital anomaly of the spine where only one half one side of a vertebral body develops It acts like a wedge inserted into one side of the spinal column leading to a congenital spinal deformity most commonly congenital scoliosis lateral curvature or kyphoscoliosis **Aetiology / Causes** Embryological Failure Occurs during the first 6 weeks of gestation It is caused by the failure of formation of one of the two lateral chondrification centers of the vertebral body Often associated with other congenital anomalies VACTERL association Vertebral Anal Cardiac Tracheal Esophageal Renal and Limb defects **Pathophysiology** A normal vertebra is shaped like a block keeping the spine straight A hemivertebra is wedge shaped Because one side of the spine has extra bony material the hemivertebra and the other side is deficient the spine curves away from the side of the hemivertebra Furthermore the hemivertebra has its own growth plates As the child grows the hemivertebra grows asymmetrically acting as a progressive wedge that continuously worsens the scoliotic curve **Classification of Hemivertebrae** Fully Segmented Has normal discs above and below it High risk of progression because both growth plates are active Semi segmented Fused to the vertebra above or below Moderate risk of progression Non segmented Fused to both the vertebra above and below Lowest risk of progression Incarcerated The hemivertebra is tucked into the spine without disrupting the overall alignment much **Clinical Features** Spinal Deformity The parents usually notice a lump on the back uneven shoulders or a visible curve in the spine scoliosis in infancy or early childhood Progression The deformity worsens rapidly during periods of growth spurts Neurological symptoms Usually absent initially Severe neglected curves can eventually cause spinal cord tethering or compression Signs Asymmetrical trunk rib hump on forward bending test Adams test **Investigations** X rays AP and Lateral of whole spine Diagnostic Shows the wedge shaped anomalous bone and the degree of scoliosis measured by Cobbs angle MRI of the Spine Mandatory to rule out associated neural axis abnormalities like tethered cord syringomyelia or diastematomyelia which are common with congenital spine defects Ultrasound Renal and Cardiac To rule out associated VACTERL anomalies **Management** Because congenital curves are rigid and do not respond to bracing management is largely observational or surgical **A Conservative Management** Observation Indicated for non segmented or incarcerated hemivertebrae that are not progressing Serial X rays every 6 months to monitor for curve progression Braces are ineffective for congenital scoliosis **B Surgical Management** Indications Documented progression of the curve fully segmented hemivertebrae in young children or severe curves at presentation Hemivertebra Excision The anomalous wedge of bone is surgically completely removed via an anterior posterior or combined approach Short Segment Spinal Fusion After excision the spine above and below the defect is brought together straightened and fused using pedicle screws and rods This corrects the deformity and prevents further asymmetric growth In Situ Fusion Fusing the spine as it is without excising the hemivertebra in very young children to arrest the growth and stop the progression of the curve","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on precise tool selection. Remembering the rules about avoiding `cat`, `grep` within `bash`, `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, and `sed`, I'm developing a systematic approach to tool usage. I'm focusing on evaluating tools before I execute them, and making sure to compare the list of tools to what is most generic or least applicable. This should lead to faster and more direct solutions.\n\n\n**Categorizing Spinal Conditions**\n\nMy focus is now on categorizing different spinal conditions starting with 1 **Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc PID** I need to organize this information systematically and clearly for easy reference This is the starting point for a more comprehensive understanding of spinal issues I plan to follow this pattern for all conditions\n\n---\n\n**Defining Spinal Issues**\n\nI have expanded the spinal conditions to include **2 Spinal Canal Stenosis** **3 Spondylosis Cervical and Lumbar** **4 Spondylolysis** and **5 Spondylolisthesis** I am continuing the process of defining and categorizing each condition to create a robust overview This expansion is key to providing a complete picture of the conditions and their differences\n\n---\n\n**Expanding Condition Listings**\n\nNow included are **6 Lumbago / Lumbosacral Strain** **7 Sacralisation Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra LSTV** **8 Lumbarisation Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra LSTV** **9 Coccydynia** and **10 Hemivertebra** The list is complete I am now moving on to outlining the conditions to make the descriptions more useful\n\n\n**Defining Spinal Conditions**\n\nMy focus has shifted to explicitly defining the first spinal condition, Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID). I've defined this as the herniation of the nucleus pulposus and identified its most common locations. Now I'm working on detailing the aetiology, or causes of PID, including age, trauma, and degeneration, and identifying common occupational hazards that contribute to it. I've cleaned up any inconsistent formatting in the text.\n\n\n**Clarifying Subtopic Structure**\n\nI'm now clarifying the subtopic structure within the definitions. I've decided to bold main headings, like \"Aetiology / Causes\", but leave the subtopics unbolded, as they aren't bulleted. The goal is to provide a clear and organized presentation of each condition. I am now working on understanding each condition as I organize it. I am refining the structure with this method.\n\n\n**Outlining Condition Progression**\n\nI'm now detailing the stages of a Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID): bulging disc, protrusion, extrusion, and sequestration. I am defining each stage in detail, which includes the condition of the annulus and nucleus. I've also incorporated the clinical features of the spinal issue and plan to explore this in detail. I'm focusing on consistency in the descriptions.\n\n\n**Detailing Physical Symptoms**\n\nNow I'm detailing the signs on examination for a Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID). I've outlined posture issues like loss of lumbar lordosis and scoliosis, along with markedly restricted spinal movements, especially forward flexion. I have included the Straight Leg Raising (SLR) Test and Lasegue's Test results. Also, I've listed possible neurological deficits for L4, L5, and S1 root compressions.\n\n\n\n**Defining Conservative Management**\n\nI'm now detailing the conservative management of Prolapsed Intervertebral Discs. I'm noting that this approach is successful in 90 percent of cases. This is indicated for patients without severe, progressive neurological deficits. I have listed \"Rest\" as a key factor and that involves strict bed rest for one to two weeks. The list includes medications like analgesics, NSAIDs for inflammation, muscle relaxants for spasm, and sometimes a short course of oral corticosteroids. I am ensuring that the formatting is consistent.\n\n\n**Outlining Surgical Options**\n\nI'm now detailing surgical management for PID, starting with specific indications like Cauda Equina Syndrome. I've outlined surgical procedures including fenestration, laminectomy and discectomy, and microdiscectomy. I'm focusing on the reasons for each, and the indications for them, ensuring that the details are complete.\n\n**Refining Condition Definition**\n\nI'm now starting on Spinal Canal Stenosis, beginning with a clear definition: it's abnormal narrowing of the spinal canal or its surrounding areas.\n\n\n**Elaborating Stenosis Details**\n\nI'm expanding the definition of Spinal Canal Stenosis to address the compression of the spinal cord in the cervical spine and the nerve roots of the cauda equina in the lumbar spine. Now I'm detailing the aetiology, distinguishing between congenital/developmental causes (like Achondroplasia) and acquired causes like degenerative changes and trauma. I'm noting the pathophysiology and causes, in line with prior formatting.\n\n\n**Describing Stenosis Features**\n\nI am now detailing the clinical features of spinal stenosis. I've described neurogenic intermittent claudication and its pathognomonic symptoms of pain in the buttocks, thighs, and calves, that appears upon walking. Relief comes with flexion. I have also described back pain, bilateral leg symptoms, and the typical clinical examination findings, including the stress test.\n\n\n**Detailing Management Strategies**\n\nI'm now detailing the management of spinal stenosis. I'm noting the imaging techniques: plain X-rays, MRI as the gold standard, and CT myelogram. I am detailing both conservative and surgical approaches: I've included lifestyle modifications, medications, physiotherapy, and epidural steroid injections. I'm focusing on surgical indications and procedures, namely, wide decompressive laminectomy.\n\n\n**Defining Spondylosis Conditions**\n\nI'm working on the third spinal condition, Spondylosis, both cervical and lumbar. I've defined it as ag