h involves vertebral displacement. I'm currently detailing aetiology including congenital weakness.\n\n\n**Elaborating Spondylolysis Details**\n\nNow, I'm elaborating on the aetiology of Spondylolysis. I've noted that congenital dysplasia of the pars makes it susceptible, and stress fractures are the most common theory, especially from repetitive hyperextension. This is common in athletes. I am also detailing the pathophysiology, the repetitive extension loads the pars, and the defect is filled with fibrous tissue. Then, I am detailing the clinical features of this condition.\n\n\n**Outlining Spondylolysis Management**\n\nI'm now detailing conservative management for Spondylolysis. I've noted the importance of activity modification, specifically avoiding hyperextension. This should include rest and analgesia. Then, I am detailing the imaging techniques used to aid diagnosis.\n\n\n**Defining Surgical Options**\n\nI'm now detailing the surgical management for Spondylolysis, covering specific procedures. This includes pars repair with screws or wiring to maintain segment motion, especially for younger patients with healthy discs. I've also detailed posterolateral fusion for cases with degeneration. I have moved onto the next spinal condition.\n\n\n**Defining Spondylolisthesis Types**\n\nI'm now detailing the types of Spondylolisthesis, removing parenthetical notations. I've covered Dysplastic, Isthmic, Degenerative, Traumatic, and Pathological types, emphasizing congenital abnormalities, stress fractures, degenerative changes, acute fractures, and bone disease as causes, respectively.\n\n\n\n**Defining Spondylolisthesis Grades**\n\nI'm now detailing the grades of Spondylolisthesis based on vertebral slip percentages, starting with Grade I (0-25%), Grade II (25-50%), Grade III (50-75%), and Grade IV (75-100%). Finally, Grade V, or spondyloptosis, involves a greater than 100% slip.\n\n**Describing Clinical Features**\n\nI'm now describing the clinical features of Spondyl