, removing unnecessary brackets and hyphens. I've clearly differentiated the role of physiotherapy and added details about local therapies. I've also clarified that surgical intervention is not indicated. I'm now transitioning to Sacralisation Lumbosacral Transitional Vertebra.\n\n\n**Defining Sacralisation**\n\nI'm now detailing Sacralisation, a congenital anomaly of the spine where L5 fuses with S1. The causes are detailed: congenital factors and genetic predisposition. The pathophysiology involves altered biomechanics leading to increased stress on L4-L5, which may require further study.\n\n\n**Detailing Sacralisation Features**\n\nI am now detailing the clinical features and investigations for sacralisation. I've described that sacralisation is often asymptomatic and found incidentally on X-rays for other reasons. Symptomatic patients often present with mechanical low back pain. I've included Bertolotti's Syndrome and localized pain. I've also listed possible clinical signs and described key investigations, using the Castellvi classification.\n\n\n**Detailing Management Strategies**\n\nI'm now detailing the management of Sacralisation, covering both conservative and surgical approaches. I've noted that reassurance is key, especially as it's a common congenital variant. Symptomatic relief includes analgesics and NSAIDs, along with physiotherapy for core strengthening. Surgical options, are very rare and only considered if conservative treatment fails. I'm focusing on the details for each condition.\n\n\n**Defining Lumbarisation Details**\n\nI am now detailing Lumbarisation, where S1 doesn't fuse completely, essentially resulting in a sixth lumbar vertebra. I'm focusing on its congenital origin, a defect in segmentation, and the resulting biomechanical instability. Premature degeneration and disc issues at the L6-S2 (or S1-S2) joint are now detailed.\n\n\n**Describing Lumbarisation Details**\n\nI'm expanding the clinical features of lumbarisation to include mechanical back pain due to altered mechanics and possible instability in the extra mobile segment. No specific clinical signs differentiate it from standard lumbago without X-rays, and the neurological exam is typically normal. I'm now documenting the key investigations, plain X-rays, and MRIs, and detailing the management plan, which parallels that of sacralisation.\n\n\n**Clarifying Coccydynia Details**\n\nI've defined Coccydynia as pain localized to the coccyx and sacrococcygeal region, clarifying causes like trauma, childbirth, and repetitive strain, and removing bracketed notes. The pathophysiology includes discussing joint movement and detailing trauma-related sprains or tears.\n\n\n**Elaborating Coccydynia Symptoms**\n\nI'm now detailing the clinical features of Coccydynia. I've listed symptoms, including severe, localized pain over the tailbone, exacerbated by sitting, especially on hard surfaces. I've added pain during the transition from sitting to standing and noted possible pain during defecation. Now, I'm documenting the signs from examinations.\n\n\n**Refining Coccydynia Details**\n\nI'm now detailing the clinical signs on examination for coccydynia. I've noted that palpation will cause localized tenderness over the coccyx. X-rays may be normal but can reveal a fracture, subluxation, or spur. Dynamic X-rays are key, as sitting versus standing can show hypermobility. Then I'm detailing management for this condition.\n\n\n\n**Defining Hemivertebra**\n\nI'm now focusing on Hemivertebra, defining it as a congenital anomaly. I'm noting its wedge shape and impact on spinal curvature, emphasizing the embryological origins from failed chondrification. I've also detailed the common associations with VACTERL and other anomalies. I'm focusing on providing more comprehensive listings.\n\n\n**Analyzing Hemivertebrae**\n\nI'm analyzing the classifications for hemivertebrae: fully, semi, non-segmented, and incarcerated types, noting their risks. I've detailed the clinical features: spinal deformity, progression, and typically absent neurological symptoms. Further analysis is needed.\n\n\n**Summarizing Hemivertebrae Management**\n\nI'm now detailing the management of Hemivertebra. I've noted that severe, neglected curves can lead to spinal cord issues. I've described signs like a trunk hump on the Adams test, and listed key investigations: X-rays, mandatory MRI to rule out neural issues, and renal/cardiac ultrasounds. Conservative management is observational, with serial X-rays, but bracing is ineffective. Surgical intervention may be necessary.\n\n\n**Refining Surgical Protocols**\n\nI'm now detailing surgical management for hemivertebrae, emphasizing that I have documented progressive curve, fully segmented hemivertebrae in young children, or severe curves at presentation. I am also detailing the surgical procedure: Hemivertebra Excision, followed by Short Segment Spinal Fusion. I'm also describing the In Situ Fusion for very young children, to arrest growth.\n\n\n**Refining PID Definition**\n\nI'm now detailing the stages of a prolapsed intervertebral disc PID including bulging disc the annulus remains intact but bulges outward and protrusion the nucleus pushes into the outer layers of the annulus but is still contained. I am expanding on the definitions to ensure a comprehensive understanding and to remain compliant with the specifications. I will be incorporating a detailed listing of the condition in each case.\n\n\n**Refining Topic Structure**\n\nI'm now clarifying the topic structure. I'm leaving \"Bulging Disc\" etc. unbolded, as they are stages, not list items. Similarly, \"Low Back Pain,\" etc. are symptoms, and are not bolded. The main topics are separated by `---` and bolded appropriately. I've corrected any text inconsistent with the requirements of the prompt.\n\n\n**Reviewing Structural Integrity**\n\nI'm now ensuring the integrity of the spinal condition listings and formatting, correcting hyphens and non-compliant characters. I've focused on restructuring with proper punctuation and bolding. I am carefully auditing the formatting of the text, removing unnecessary line breaks and inconsistent symbols. I aim to create a well-structured document, following the specifications.\n\n\n**Refining Detailed Listings**\n\nI'm now detailing each spinal condition's elements, removing unnecessary spaces and periods, following the provided structure. I am making comprehensive listings of symptoms, treatments, and management strategies. I'm focusing on consistency. For example, I have listed \"90 percent of cases\" and \"1 2 weeks\". I'm making sure all the numbers are represented correctly, such as \"0 25 percent\". I'm removing all unnecessary text.\n\n\n"}
