{"step_index":7,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:41:15Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nPART I — SHOULDER Regional Conditions of the Shoulder — 20-Mark University Exam Answer Introduction The shoulder is a highly mobile joint complex consisting mainly of the glenohumeral, acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular and scapulothoracic articulations. Because of its wide range of movement and dependence on soft tissues for stability, it is commonly affected by painful disorders involving the joint capsule, rotator cuff tendons, biceps tendon and subacromial bursa. The important regional shoulder conditions include: 1. Periarthritis shoulder / Adhesive capsulitis 2. Rotator cuff tendinitis 3. Supraspinatus tendinitis 4. Infraspinatus tendinitis 5. Bicipital tendinitis 6. Subacromial bursitis 1. Periarthritis Shoulder / Adhesive Capsulitis Definition Adhesive capsulitis is a painful condition of the shoulder characterized by progressive pain, stiffness and marked restriction of both active and passive glenohumeral movements, particularly external rotation. It is commonly called frozen shoulder. Clinical Features • Gradual onset of shoulder pain. • Pain is often worse at night and may disturb sleep. • Progressive stiffness of the shoulder. • Difficulty with combing hair, dressing, reaching overhead and reaching behind the back. • Both active and passive ROM are restricted. • External rotation is usually the most restricted movement, followed by abduction and internal rotation. • Capsular pattern: External rotation > abduction > internal rotation. • Muscle weakness may develop secondary to pain and disuse. Page 1 BPT University Exam Notes• Scapular movements may become excessive as compensation. Stages 1. Freezing stage: increasing pain and progressive loss of movement. 2. Frozen stage: pain may decrease but marked stiffness persists. 3. Thawing stage: gradual recovery of ROM and function. Management Medical management • Patient education and activity modification. • Analgesics and NSAIDs as prescribed. • Corticosteroid injection, particularly in the painful early stage. • Hydrodilatation in selected patients. • Manipulation under anesthesia or arthroscopic capsular release in resistant cases. Physiotherapy management • Pain-relieving modalities as appropriate. • Gentle pendular exercises. • Gentle active-assisted ROM. • Avoid aggressive stretching that increases pain. • Joint mobilization and capsular stretching. • Active and active-assisted ROM. • Wand/pulley exercises. • Scapular mobility exercises. • Progressive stretching and strengthening. • Rotator cuff and scapular muscle strengthening. • Functional and home exercise programme. 2. Rotator Cuff Tendinitis Definition Rotator cuff tendinitis is an inflammatory or degenerative condition involving one or more tendons of the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis, commonly producing shoulder pain during elevation and rotation. The supraspinatus tendon is most frequently involved. Clinical Features • Pain over the shoulder, commonly around the anterolateral aspect. • Pain increases with overhead activities. • Painful arc during shoulder abduction, commonly between 60° and 120°. Page 2 BPT University Exam Notes• Pain may increase at night. • Weakness due to pain and inhibition. • Restricted active movement may occur. • Passive ROM is usually relatively preserved. • Tenderness over the affected tendon. • Positive rotator cuff provocation tests may be present. • Difficulty with lifting, reaching and overhead activities. Management • Relative rest and activity modification. • Avoid repetitive overhead activities. • Ice during the painful phase. • Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Gentle ROM and pendulum exercises. • Stretching. • Rotator cuff strengthening. • Scapular stabilizer strengthening. • Progressive resisted exercises. • Postural correction. • Gradual return to overhead activities. • Specialist assessment for persistent symptoms or structural pathology. 3. Supraspinatus Tendinitis Definition Supraspinatus tendinitis is an inflammatory or degenerative disorder of the supraspinatus tendon, usually occurring near its passage beneath the acromion, causing pain particularly during shoulder elevation. Clinical Features • Pain over the anterolateral aspect of the shoulder. • Pain increases during abduction and overhead activities. • Painful arc, commonly from approximately 60°–120°. • Pain may be worse at night. • Tenderness over the supraspinatus tendon. • Pain or weakness during resisted abduction. • Difficulty lifting the arm. • Positive empty-can/Jobe test may reproduce pain or weakness. • Passive ROM is generally less affected than active ROM. • Severe cases may produce weakness of abduction. Page 3 BPT University Exam NotesManagement • Relative rest from painful overhead activities. • Ice during acute painful periods. • Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Gentle ROM exercises. • Stretching. • Progressive supraspinatus and rotator cuff strengthening. • Isometric → isotonic → functional strengthening. • Scapular stabilization exercises. • Postural correction. • Gradual return to overhead activities. • Corticosteroid injection may be considered in selected cases. • Persistent or associated structural lesions may require specialist assessment. 4. Infraspinatus Tendinitis Definition Infraspinatus tendinitis is inflammation or degeneration of the infraspinatus tendon, resulting in pain and dysfunction mainly during external rotation of the shoulder. Clinical Features • Posterior shoulder pain. • Pain may radiate toward the lateral arm. • Pain increases during external rotation. • Pain during overhead activities. • Tenderness over the posterior shoulder/infraspinatus tendon. • Pain or weakness on resisted external rotation. • Difficulty with activities requiring external rotation. • Weakness may occur because of pain. • Passive ROM is usually relatively preserved. Management • Relative rest and avoidance of aggravating activities. • Ice for pain where appropriate. • Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Gentle shoulder ROM. • Stretching exercises. • Isometric external rotation exercises initially. • Progressive resisted external rotation. Page 4 BPT University Exam Notes• Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening. • Postural correction. • Gradual functional and sports-specific rehabilitation. 5. Bicipital Tendinitis Definition Bicipital tendinitis is inflammation or degenerative irritation of the tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii, particularly within the intertubercular/bicipital groove. Clinical Features • Pain in the anterior shoulder. • Pain may radiate down the anterior aspect of the arm. • Pain increases with shoulder flexion, elbow flexion, forearm supination and overhead activities. • Tenderness over the bicipital groove. • Pain on resisted elbow flexion. • Pain on resisted forearm supination. • Positive Speed's test may reproduce symptoms. • Yergason's test may also reproduce pain. • Weakness may occur due to pain. • Night pain can occur. • Difficulty lifting objects or performing repetitive overhead activities. Management • Relative rest and activity modification. • Avoid repetitive lifting and overhead activities. • Ice during painful periods. • Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Gentle shoulder ROM and stretching. • Progressive biceps strengthening after pain decreases. • Rotator cuff and scapular stabilization. • Postural correction. • Gradual return to functional activities. • Injection or surgical management may be considered depending on underlying pathology. 6. Subacromial Bursitis Definition Page 5 BPT University Exam NotesSubacromial bursitis is inflammation of the subacromial bursa, a fluid-filled structure that reduces friction between the rotator cuff tendons and the acromion during shoulder movement. Clinical Features • Shoulder pain, commonly over the lateral/anterior shoulder. • Pain increases with overhead activities. • Painful arc, often around 60°–120° of abduction. • Night pain may be present. • Tenderness around the subacromial region. • Pain with active shoulder elevation. • Difficulty reaching overhead. • Difficulty dressing and grooming. • Muscle guarding may occur. • Weakness may be present due to pain inhibition. Management Medical management • Relative rest. • Activity modification. • Ice. • Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Corticosteroid injection may be considered in selected cases. Physiotherapy management • Pain-relieving modalities when indicated. • Gentle ROM and pendular exercises. • Stretching. • Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening. • Postural correction. • Correction of movement patterns. • Progressive functional exercises. • Gradual return to overhead activities. Comparison of Important Clinical Features • Adhesive capsulitis: joint capsule; restriction of both active and passive ROM. • Rotator cuff tendinitis: rotator cuff tendons; pain with elevation/overhead activity. • Supraspinatus tendinitis: supraspinatus tendon; painful arc and painful resisted abduction. • Infraspinatus tendinitis: infraspinatus tendon; pain with resisted external rotation. • Bicipital tendinitis: long head of biceps; anterior shoulder pain and bicipital groove tenderness. Page 6 BPT University Exam Notes• Subacromial bursitis: subacromial bursa; painful elevation/abduction. General Physiotherapy Principles 1. Pain management: cryotherapy where appropriate, heat in selected chronic/stiff conditions, adjunctive electrotherapy where indicated, and activity modification. 2. Restoration of ROM: pendular, active-assisted, wand and pulley exercises; capsular stretching in adhesive capsulitis. 3. Strengthening: isometric → isotonic → resisted → functional strengthening. 4. Scapular rehabilitation: retraction, depression, controlled upward rotation, serratus anterior and lower trapezius strengthening. 5. Postural correction. 6. Functional rehabilitation: ADLs, overhead activity, work-specific and sports-specific exercises. Conclusion Regional shoulder conditions commonly involve the capsule, rotator cuff tendons, biceps tendon and subacromial bursa. Careful assessment of pain location, painful arc, active and passive ROM, resisted movements and special tests helps differentiate them. Management is primarily conservative and includes pain control, activity modification, restoration of mobility, progressive strengthening, scapular stabilization, postural correction and functional rehabilitation. Page 7 BPT University Exam NotesPART II — ELBOW, WRIST & HAND, PELVIS & HIP, KNEE, ANKLE & FOOT Regional Orthopaedic Conditions — 20-Mark University Examination Answers ELBOW 1. Tennis Elbow — Lateral Epicondylitis Definition Tennis elbow is a painful overuse disorder of the tendons originating from the common extensor origin at the lateral epicondyle of the humerus, most commonly involving the extensor carpi radialis brevis. It is usually a tendinopathy rather than a purely inflammatory condition. Etiology/Risk Factors • Repetitive wrist extension. • Repetitive gripping. • Racquet sports. • Manual work. • Repetitive computer or occupational activities. • Sudden increase in activity. • Poor technique during sports. Clinical Features • Pain over the lateral epicondyle, sometimes radiating down the lateral forearm. • Pain aggravated by gripping, wrist extension, lifting, turning a screwdriver and shaking hands. • Tenderness over the lateral epicondyle/common extensor origin. • Reduced grip strength due to pain. • Pain on resisted wrist extension. • Difficulty performing occupational activities. Investigations • Mainly clinical. • X-ray to exclude bony pathology. • Ultrasound/MRI in persistent or unclear cases. Management • Activity modification and relative rest. • Ice during painful periods. Page 8 BPT University Exam Notes• Analgesics/NSAIDs as prescribed. • Counterforce brace or wrist splint in selected cases. • Wrist/forearm stretching. • Isometric wrist extension. • Progressive eccentric/concentric extensor strengthening. • Grip strengthening. • Manual therapy. • Ergonomic correction. • Gradual return to sport/work. 2. Golfer's Elbow — Medial Epicondylitis Definition Golfer's elbow is a tendinopathy involving the common flexor-pronator tendon origin at the medial epicondyle of the humerus, producing medial elbow pain. Etiology • Repetitive wrist flexion. • Repetitive forearm pronation. • Golf. • Throwing sports. • Manual occupations. • Repetitive gripping. Clinical Features • Pain over medial epicondyle, possibly radiating down medial forearm. • Tenderness at common flexor origin. • Pain during wrist flexion, forearm pronation and gripping. • Reduced grip strength. • Pain on resisted wrist flexion/pronation. • Occasionally ulnar nerve symptoms may coexist. Management • Activity modification, ice and analgesics as prescribed. • Brace if indicated. • Stretching of wrist flexors. • Isometric → progressive resisted strengthening. • Grip strengthening. • Eccentric training. • Manual therapy. Page 9 BPT University Exam Notes• Ergonomic correction. • Gradual return to activity. 3. Olecranon Bursitis — Student's Elbow Definition Olecranon bursitis is inflammation of the olecranon bursa, located between the skin and olecranon process of the ulna. It is commonly called student's elbow because prolonged leaning on the elbows can irritate the bursa. Causes • Repeated pressure. • Trauma. • Prolonged elbow leaning. • Infection. • Gout or inflammatory arthritis. Clinical Features • Swelling over posterior elbow. • Soft/fluctuant swelling over olecranon. • Pain or tenderness. • Warmth and redness if inflamed/infected. • Usually preserved elbow ROM. • Septic cases may cause fever and systemic symptoms. Investigations • Clinical examination. • Aspiration and fluid analysis when infection/gout is suspected. • Blood investigations in suspected infection. • Imaging when necessary. Management Non-infective: avoid pressure, activity modification, ice, compression/padding, analgesics and selected aspiration; recurrent cases may require surgery. Septic bursitis: medical assessment, antibiotics and drainage/aspiration when indicated. Physiotherapy: protection, gentle ROM, avoidance of direct pressure, and gradual strengthening after acute symptoms settle. 4. Triceps Tendinitis Definition Page 10 BPT University Exam NotesTriceps tendinitis is an overuse tendinopathy affecting the distal triceps tendon near its insertion into the olecranon, causing posterior elbow pain. Causes • Repetitive elbow extension. • Weightlifting. • Throwing. • Repetitive pushing. • Sudden increase in exercise load. Clinical Features • Posterior elbow pain. • Tenderness near triceps insertion. • Pain during resisted elbow extension. • Pain during pushing activities. • Weakness due to pain. • Pain after repetitive activity. • Occasionally swelling. Management • Relative rest and ice. • Activity modification. • Analgesics/NSAIDs. • Gentle ROM. • Triceps stretching. • Isometric exercises initially. • Progressive resisted strengthening. • Eccentric loading. • Gradual return to sport/work. WRIST AND HAND 5. De Quervain's Tenosynovitis Definition De Quervain's tenosynovitis is a painful stenosing tenosynovitis affecting the first dorsal compartment of the wrist, involving the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons. Causes/Risk Factors • Repetitive thumb movements. Page 11 BPT University Exam Notes• Repetitive wrist motion. • Lifting infants. • Occupational overuse. • Repetitive gripping. Clinical Features • Pain over radial side of wrist, possibly radiating toward thumb/forearm. • Swelling over first dorsal compartment. • Pain with thumb movement. • Pain during gripping and lifting. • Reduced grip strength. • Tenderness over radial styloid. • Positive Finkelstein test. Management • Activity modification. • Thumb-spica splint. • Ice. • Analgesics/NSAIDs. • Corticosteroid injection when indicated. • Gentle ROM, thumb exercises, tendon gliding, stretching and progressive strengthening. • Surgical release in persistent cases. 6. Ganglion Definition A ganglion is a benign cystic swelling containing gelatinous fluid, commonly arising from a joint capsule or tendon sheath, particularly around the wrist. Clinical Features • Smooth rounded swelling, commonly dorsal wrist. • May change in size. • May be painless. • Pain may occur with wrist movement or pressure. • Possible weakness or discomfort. • Usually firm/fluctuant. • May transilluminate. Investigations • Clinical examination. Page 12 BPT University Exam Notes• Ultrasound. • MRI when diagnosis is uncertain. Management • Observation if asymptomatic. • Activity modification. • Splinting for painful cases. • Aspiration in selected cases. • Surgical excision for recurrent/symptomatic ganglion. • Physiotherapy for pain, ROM, strengthening, functional modification and scar management after surgery. 7. Trigger Finger / Trigger Thumb Definition Trigger finger is a stenosing tenosynovitis in which thickening of the flexor tendon/pulley system causes difficulty in smooth tendon gliding, producing catching, locking or snapping during finger movement. Clinical Features • Pain/tenderness at volar MCP region. • Clicking or snapping. • Finger catches during flexion/extension. • Locking in flexion in advanced cases. • Morning stiffness. • Palpable flexor tendon nodule. • Difficulty opening/closing the hand. Management • Activity modification. • Rest. • Splinting. • Analgesics. • Tendon-gliding exercises. • Gentle ROM. • Corticosteroid injection when indicated. • Surgical A1 pulley release for persistent/severe cases. • Postoperative scar management and mobilization. 8. Mallet Finger Definition Page 13 BPT University Exam NotesMallet finger is an injury to the terminal extensor mechanism at the distal interphalangeal joint, usually resulting in inability to actively extend the DIP joint. Causes • Sudden forced flexion of an actively extended finger. • Sports injuries. • Avulsion fracture may occur. Clinical Features • Drooping fingertip. • Inability to actively extend DIP. • Pain and swelling may occur. • Passive extension is usually possible. • Chronic cases may develop swan-neck deformity. Investigations • X-ray to identify avulsion fracture and joint involvement. Management • DIP joint splinting in extension. • Continuous splinting for the prescribed period. • Maintain PIP mobility. • Surgical treatment in selected large/displaced fractures or joint instability. • Physiotherapy: splint compliance, PIP/MCP mobility, gradual DIP ROM after immobilization and progressive strengthening. 9. Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Definition Carpal tunnel syndrome is a compressive neuropathy of the median nerve at the wrist as it passes through the carpal tunnel. Causes/Risk Factors • Repetitive wrist use. • Diabetes. • Pregnancy. • Rheumatoid arthritis. • Hypothyroidism. • Wrist trauma. • Space-occupying lesions. Page 14 BPT University Exam Notes• Increased pressure within 